stract\n\nA set L of linear polynomials in variables with real coefficients is said to be an essential cover of the cube if\n\n  * (E1)\n\nfor each , there is a such that ;\n\n  * (E2)\n\nno proper subset of L satisfies (E1), that is, for every , there is a such that p alone takes the value 0 on v;\n\n  * (E3)\n\nevery variable appears (in some monomial with non-zero coefficient) in some polynomial of L.\n\nLet be the size of the smallest essential cover of . In the present note we show that--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBenny Sudakov: *SDP, MaxCut, discrepancy and log-rank-conjecture* (https://bernoulli.epfl.ch/comb2024/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/workshop_schedule_and_abstracts.pdf)\nciteturn0search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; Benny Sudakov: *SDP, MaxCut, discrepancy and log-rank-conjecture* ... Zixuan Xu: *Essential covers of the hypercube requires many hyperplanes*  \nWe prove a new lower bound for the smallest possible size of an essential cover of the n-dimensional hypercube, i.e. the smallest possible size of a collection of hyperplanes that forms a minimal cover and such that furthermore every variable appears with a non-zero coefficient in at least one of the hyperplane equations.\nJoint work with Nidhi Rathi (https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.14463)\n\nZixuan Xu: *Essential covers of the hypercube requires many hyperplanes*  \nWe prove a new lower bound for the smallest possible size of an essential cover of the n-dimensional hypercube, i.e. the smallest possible size of a collection of hyperplanes that forms a minimal cover and such that furthermore every variable appears with a non-zero coefficient in at least one of the hyperplane equations. We show that such an essential cover must contain at least Ω(n^(2/3)/(log n)^(2/3)) hyperplanes, improving previous lower bounds of Linial–Radhakrishnan, of Yehuda–Yehudayoff and of Araujo-Balogh–Mattos. This new lower bound also implies new lower bounds for some problems in proof complexity. Based on joint work with Lisa Sauermann.\n\nSammy Luo: *A New Polynomial Method in Additive Combinatorics*  \nA central tool in additive combinatorics is the “polynomial method”, a family of powerful techniques for studying the existence and size of sets satisfying given properties by encoding them in terms of the zeros of certain polynomials, which can then be analyzed from an algebraic perspective.  \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExtremal Hyperplane Problems on the Hypercube (https://dspace.mit.edu/entities/publication/b311a1d0-cedb-4d6e-8169-e6a512260bb3)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: last month; A collection of (affine) hyperplanes H in R n is a cover of the n-dimensional hypercube {0, 1}^n if for every vertex v ∈ {0, 1}^n , there exists a hyperplane H ∈ H such that v ∈ H. ... Another well-studied type of hyperplane cover is called essential covers, where every variable must appear with nonzero coefficient in at least one of the hyperplanes and every hyperplane must cover a vertex that is not covered by any other hyperplane. ... We confirm the conjecture in the case where the hyperplanes have bounded integer coefficients {−C, . . . , C} for some constant C > 0, generalizing a result of Alon–Bergmann–Coppersmith–Odlyzko.\nHowever, such an answer is not satisfactory since such a hyperplane cover is degenerate in the sense that none of the other variables appear in any of the hyperplane equations. Therefore, it is natural to impose certain nondegeneracy condition on the hyperplane cover. One type of nondegenerate covers is called skew covers where the normal vector of every hyperplane in the collection must have full support. We obtain an asymptotically tight lower bound for the minimum size of a skew cover up to constant factors. Furthermore, we strengthen our lower bound result to a weaker nondegeneracy condition. Another well-studied type of hyperplane cover is called essential covers, where every variable must appear with nonzero coefficient in at least one of the hyperplanes and every hyperplane must cover a vertex that is not covered by any other hyperplane. We prove that any essential cover must contain at least Ω(n^2/3 / (log n) ^2/3 ) hyperplanes, improving on previous results of Linial–Radhakrishnan, Yehuda–Yehudayoff and Araujo–Balogh–Mattos. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEssential covers of the hypercube require many hyperplanes - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2310.05775v1)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; We prove a new lower bound for the almost 20 year old problem of determining the smallest possible size of an essential cover of the $n$-dimensional hypercube $\\{\\pm 1\\}^n$, i.e. the smallest possible size of a collection of hyperplanes that forms a minimal cover of $\\{\\pm 1\\}^n$ and such that furthermore every variable appears with a non-zero coefficient in at least one of the hyperplane equations.\n\n## Essential covers of the hypercube require many hyperplanes\n\nLisa Sauermann, Zixuan Xu\n\nPublished: 2023-10-09\n\nNote:\n\nJournal: Combinator. Probab. Comp. 34 (2025) 326-337\n\nDOI: 10.1017/S0963548324000257\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe prove a new lower bound for the almost 20 year old problem of determining the smallest possible size of an essential cover of the $n$-dimensional hypercube $\\{\\pm 1\\}^n$, i.e. the smallest possible size of a collection of hyperplanes that forms a minimal cover of $\\{\\pm 1\\}^n$ and such that furthermore every variable appears with a non-zero coefficient in at least one of the hyperplane equations. We show that such an essential cover must consist of at least $10^{-2}\\cdot n^{2/3}/(\\log n)^{2/3}$ hyperplanes, improving previous lower bounds of Linial-Radhakrishnan, of Yehuda-Yehudayoff and of Araujo-Balogh-Mattos.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNew Lower Bounds For Essential Covers Of The Cube - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2209.00140v1)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; An essential cover of the vertices of the $n$-cube $\\{0,1\\}^n$ by hyperplanes is a minimal covering where no hyperplane is redundant and every variable appears in the equation of at least one hyperplane.\n\n## New Lower Bounds For Essential Covers Of The Cube\n\nIgor Araujo, József Balogh, Letícia Mattos\n\nPublished: 2022-08-31\n\nComments: Extended version of the submitted paper, 21 pages, 3 figures\n\nNote:\n\nDOI: 10.1007/s11856-024-2695-9\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nAn essential cover of the vertices of the $n$-cube $\\{0,1\\}^n$ by hyperplanes is a minimal covering where no hyperplane is redundant and every variable appears in the equation of at least one hyperplane. Linial and Radhakrishnan gave a construction of an essential cover with $\\lceil \\frac{n}{2} \\rceil + 1$ hyperplanes and showed that $Ω(\\sqrt{n})$ hyperplanes are required. Recently, Yehuda and Yehudayoff improved the lower bound by showing that any essential cover of the $n$-cube contains at least $Ω(n^{0.52})$ hyperplanes. In this paper, building on the method of Yehuda and Yehudayoff, we prove that $Ω\\left( \\frac{n^{5/9}}{(\\log n)^{4/9}} \\right)$ hyperplanes are needed.\n\n### BibTeX\n\n[Button: arXiv BibTeX ] [Button: Published BibTeX ]\n\nMaintained by Per Alexandersson, per@symmetricfunctions.com--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNondegenerate hyperplane covers of the hypercube (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v33i2p6/pdf/)\nciteturn0search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; with normal vectors in {1, −1}n for which the conjecture was previously 